  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given an array AAA consisting of NNN integers. You are allowed to **rearrange** the elements of the array in any order.\n\nA segment [l,r][l, r][l,r] of the array is considered beautiful if, for **every** pair (i,j)(i, j)(i,j) where l≤i≤j≤rl \\leq i \\leq j \\leq rl≤i≤j≤r, either Aimod  Aj=0A_i \\mod A_j = 0Ai​modAj​=0 or Ajmod  Ai=0A_j \\mod A_i = 0Aj​modAi​=0. Note that `mod` denotes the modulo operation.\n\nThe score of a beautiful segment [l,r][l, r][l,r] is calculated as follows:\n\n\n`score`([l,r])=min⁡(Al,Al+1,…,Ar)⋅(r−l+1)([l, r]) = \\min(A_l, A_{l + 1}, \\ldots, A_r) \\cdot (r - l + 1)([l,r])=min(Al​,Al+1​,…,Ar​)⋅(r−l+1)\nYour task is to determine the **maximum possible score** of a beautiful segment within the given array AAA.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nThe first line of each test case contains an integer NNN - the length of the array AAA.\nThe second line of each test case contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1,A_2,\\ldots,A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​, denoting the array.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line, the maximum possible score of a beautiful segment.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N,Ai≤1051 \\leq N, A_i \\leq 10^51≤N,Ai​≤105",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 3⋅1053 \\cdot 10^53⋅105.",
      "The sum of max⁡(Ai)\\max(A_i)max(Ai​) over all test cases won't exceed 3⋅1053 \\cdot 10^53⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n4\n2 3 4 6\n5\n4 4 8 10 4",
        "output": "6\n16",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: The array elements can be rearranged as [3,6,2,4][3, 6, 2, 4][3,6,2,4].\nThe beautiful segments are [1,1],[2,2],[3,3],[4,4],[1,2],[2,3],[3,4][1,1], [2,2], [3,3], [4,4], [1,2], [2,3], [3, 4][1,1],[2,2],[3,3],[4,4],[1,2],[2,3],[3,4].\nscore([1,2])=min⁡(A1,A2)⋅2=6([1, 2]) = \\min(A_1, A_2) \\cdot 2 = 6([1,2])=min(A1​,A2​)⋅2=6, which is the maximum possible score.\nTest case 222: The array elements can be rearranged as [4,4,8,4,10][4, 4, 8, 4, 10][4,4,8,4,10].\nThe beautiful segment [1,4][1, 4][1,4] has the maximum score of 161616."
      }
    ]
